{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head d84a0a25de057a4bec5b107869a3f558b838c246. Reviewed all 44 declared artifacts, including the publisher container, durable landing core, workspace provisioning, diff engine, tests, deployment configuration, and dated measurement evidence.\n\nArchitecture: the durable single-slot reservation and retained integration/expected-main pair are sound foundations. Existing tests cover delayed accepted pushes, lost responses, restart recovery, parallel preparation, and compromise handling. A readiness response race still prevents reliable progress, and merge-base selection can attribute inherited changes to a lane.\n\nSecurity: the git-only publisher and hook disabling preserve the intended trust boundary. Workspace provisioning must positively establish fork provenance and enforce the lease deadline on the actual remote credential. The in-flight token measurements honestly distinguish a push withheld before authentication from a push whose body had already arrived; they do not justify treating revocation, expiry, or elapsed time as proof that an unresolved push cannot land.\n\nSimplification: retain the narrow Room interface and current reservation boundary. Fix these cases within the existing state machine and bounded diff implementation; no reduction of the planned scope is needed.\n\n1. P1 — An older readiness response can permanently strand a ready landing (src/landing/engine.ts:148–163; src/landing/core.ts:441–464). Two evaluations of the same attempt and integration can overlap. In an independent test, a newer ready answer resolves first, then an older waiting answer changes the op back to preparing. applyReadiness deletes readinessPending, and the waiting result does not schedule another evaluation. nextDue is null and reconcile leaves the op stuck, although the required checks have already arrived. Bind responses to a durable evaluation revision, or serialize/coalesce evaluations with a guaranteed follow-up when evidence changes. Older waiting, failed, retry, or error answers must not replace newer readiness. Add tests for both reply orders, evidence arriving during evaluation, and restart, while keeping synchronous reservation revalidation.\n\n2. P1 — A non-fork repository is accepted as a lane workspace (src/workspace/workspaces.ts:272). getFork rejects a mismatched non-null source but accepts source:null. An independent test pre-populates the deterministic lane repository name with a plain repository; provisioning reports ready and mints a write credential without creating a fork. The suffix check also does not establish the exact canonical namespace. Require positive provenance from the configured canonical repository, refusing plain repositories and repositories from a different origin. Do not overwrite or delete a colliding unrelated repository. Test null source, a different canonical repository, another namespace with the same basename, and legitimate reuse after a lost fork response.\n\n3. P1 — Token creation can issue a credential beyond the lease deadline (src/workspace/workspaces.ts:206–238,291–312). TTL is calculated before awaited revocation and token creation. After minting, the current row is checked for generation and state, but not current lease expiry or the token's actual expiry. With a 120-second lease and a 30-second mint delay, the independent fake remote returns a token valid for 30 seconds beyond the lease; grant masks this by advertising the earlier deadline. With a 180-second delay, provisioning still becomes ready and grant returns a live credential with an already expired advertised deadline. Re-read the current lease after awaits, reject expired leases in grant, and validate the actual returned token expiry against the current lease. Revoke stale or overlong tokens, retaining durable cleanup on revocation failure. Retry with the remaining TTL only when a valid token can fit the service minimum. Add both delayed-mint cases alongside release and lease-handoff races.\n\n4. P2 — Clock skew leaves a redundant merge base and adds inherited paths (src/diff/treediff.ts:254). The paint-down traversal orders by committedAt and can stop before stale propagation removes an ancestor already marked RESULT. A real Git reproduction has root R; A descends from R, M from A, B from M, and C from R. Both tips have parents A, B, C; B's date precedes M's. Git merge-base --all returns B and C, while this implementation returns A, B and C. B and C already contain shared.txt's change, and the lane edits only lane.txt. The candidate therefore reports shared.txt as another lane change. This can refuse legitimate scope or demand unrelated review. Remove ancestor candidates independently of wall-clock ordering, within the existing maxCommits bound. Compare with Git on backward and equal timestamps and verify the final changed paths. Preserve the union over genuinely distinct best bases.\n\n5. P2 — Bounded diff refusal depends on asynchronous completion order (src/diff/treediff.ts, treeDiff shared stop result). An independent test uses the same immutable trees with deep and wide branches that violate both depth and entry bounds. Delaying the wide read produces a depth refusal; delaying the deep read produces an entries refusal. Cache warmth can change that ordering too. The documented deterministic refusal contract requires stable admission/refusal details for identical input and bounds. Use deterministic traversal/counting or a stable refusal priority while preserving bounded work and concurrent reads where safe. Test cold and warm caches and opposite response delays when multiple limits are crossed.\n\nValidation: candidate typechecking passed; all 76 Node tests and 8 local workerd tests passed. Seven isolated diagnostic tests passed, including the independent ancestry oracle and the six reproduced defects/cases above. The real Git ancestry reproduction and local fake-service tests ran without live credentials. I reviewed both live measurement recordings and the revocation/expiry recordings; I did not rerun live measurement or deploy anything. Existing measurement results show two successful disjoint landings, a content-conflict refusal, cold/warm diff reads, a bounded refusal, and workspace token refusal after release. These findings concern correctness and credential lifetime, not schedule estimates.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0b90d89fd85009fb9eea02b883e62c316fd5a53b","binding":"assigned","head":"d84a0a25de057a4bec5b107869a3f558b838c246","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1d9c23ac4e51ca66b500dd4f76ae6947464ce728\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f1c712fd424d1444df42240b8eeae16c41626171\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bf17b0654beb0b883f5c4f9b22ba4aa1d2e692fd","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}